WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.875
The approach is first to carefully separate the two
possible causes of the oscillations.

00:00:05.955 --> 00:00:11.086
Is it a statistical problem, meaning not enough
data, or a computational one,

00:00:11.166 --> 00:00:14.334
meaning something about the optimization itself?

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:19.612
By ablating away the statistical difficulty and
still seeing the oscillations,

00:00:19.692 --> 00:00:23.740
the authors pin the blame on gradient variance
amplification.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.973
Then they test the natural fixes.

00:00:26.053 --> 00:00:29.086
Aggressively decaying the learning rate helps,

00:00:29.166 --> 00:00:34.567
and dramatically increasing the batch size helps,
but both cost a lot of compute.

00:00:34.647 --> 00:00:37.139
The star of the paper is much cheaper.

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:41.808
You simply keep a running exponential moving
average of your weights,

00:00:41.888 --> 00:00:43.364
updating it every step,

00:00:43.444 --> 00:00:49.184
defined by theta-tilde at time t plus one equals
one minus gamma times the old average

00:00:49.264 --> 00:00:51.417
plus gamma times the new iterate.

00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:56.154
A supporting theorem shows that in a simple
deterministic environment,

00:00:56.234 --> 00:01:02.177
a tiny perturbation that barely changes the
imitation loss can blow up the rollout reward

00:01:02.257 --> 00:01:04.410
gap exponentially in the horizon.
